\details{
The validation profile is explicitly \code{"google-parser-compatible"}. It
reports what the vendored parser recognizes (including the parser's accepted
typo and missing-colon variants) and adds conservative structural checks. It
is not a claim of universal crawler behavior or complete RFC 9309 validity.

\code{robots_txt} may be text or a raw vector. Raw input is useful when validating
acquisition bytes that contain malformed UTF-8, a byte-order mark, or NUL
bytes. Character input marked UTF-8 is used as written. Unmarked input (as
\code{rawToChar()} gives) is taken as UTF-8 bytes as written when the session's
native encoding is UTF-8 or ASCII (the C locale), and converted from the
native encoding otherwise; input marked \code{"latin1"} is converted, and input
marked \code{Encoding = "bytes"} is used verbatim.

The result contains three typed data frames: \code{map} (the validation target),
\code{documents} (one summary row per document), and \code{diagnostics} (zero or more
findings). Diagnostic severities are \code{"info"}, \code{"warning"}, and \code{"error"};
document statuses are \code{"valid"}, \code{"warning"}, \code{"error"}, and
\code{"not_validated"}. An info never changes the status, so a document whose
only finding is \code{no_rules} is \code{"valid"}.

In \code{documents}, \code{encoding} says only whether the bytes are valid UTF-8
(\code{"utf-8"} or \code{"invalid-utf-8"}); a NUL byte counts as valid, so UTF-16
ASCII text can read \code{"utf-8"}. \code{encoding_signature} names the encoding the
bytes point to: \code{"utf-8-bom"}, \code{"utf-16le"}, \code{"utf-16be"}, \code{"utf-32le"},
\code{"utf-32be"}, or \code{NA}. \code{encoding_signature_basis} says how: \code{"bom"} for a
byte-order mark, \code{"byte_pattern"} for the heuristic that reads the zero
bytes of ASCII text in UTF-16 or UTF-32. The signature describes a raw or
fetched body; character input, even marked \code{"bytes"}, is text the caller
already decoded, so both are \code{NA} for it. \code{has_bom} can still flag a
leading byte-order mark character in character input while
\code{encoding_signature} stays \code{NA}.
}
