For \code{accepted_directive_prefix}, Google's parser accepts any key that
starts with \code{allow} or \code{disallow}, ignoring ASCII case, so
\verb{DisallowAITraining: /} is read as \verb{Disallow: /} and blocks the group's
crawlers. The Bing and Yandex matchers compare the whole field name and
ignore the line, and RFC 9309's grammar (section 2.2) has only \code{allow} and
\code{disallow} rule records, leaving other records to the crawler (section
2.2.4). The message names the key as written; for a Disallow key it points
to a separate \verb{User-agent: __usage_ai-training} group, not stacked under
another User-agent line, as the AI-training opt-out.
An accepted typo such as \code{Dissallow} is reported as
\code{accepted_directive_typo} only.

For \code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited}, a crawler obeys only the groups that
name it (combined when several do) and ignores the \verb{User-agent: *} group,
as RFC 9309 section 2.2.1 says. Each \code{*} Allow or Disallow is compared
with the named crawler's rules by type and value, except that a \code{*} Allow
matching every path is not listed, because the named group's own rules are
what the crawler follows. Such an Allow is \code{/} with any \code{*}s after it, or a
run of \code{*}s with an optional \code{/} before and an optional final \code{$} (\code{/},
\verb{/*}, \code{*}, \verb{/*$}, \verb{*$}; \verb{/$} is not one). A trailing \code{*} is ignored (\verb{/x}
equals \verb{/x*}, and \code{*} and \verb{/*} equal \code{/}), \code{$} is not, and empty values are
left out. The message lists the missing \code{*} rules, ten at most. A crawler
whose rules are all explicit allow-all (an empty \verb{Disallow:} or an
every-path \code{Allow}, as above) is not reported; an empty \verb{Allow:} is ignored,
and a crawler whose groups have no other rule is reported. A crawler whose
merged groups disallow \code{/} (a \code{Disallow} of \code{/}, \verb{/*}, \code{*}, \verb{**} or \verb{/**},
not one ending in \code{$}) and hold no non-empty \code{Allow} is in a full block:
the \code{*} Disallows it lacks add nothing, so only its lost \code{*} Allow rules are
listed, as rules that may be meant as exceptions. An empty \verb{Allow:} does not
cancel a full block.

A lost \code{*} Disallow is a \code{wildcard_disallows_not_inherited} warning when a
path it matches (the pattern with each \code{*} as nothing or \code{x}, and unless it
ends in \code{$}, with \code{x} or \verb{/x} appended) is disallowed under the \code{*} groups
and allowed by the Disallows of the crawler's merged groups alone, as
Google's matcher reads both; a \code{*} Allow that reopens every such path
leaves no warning, and a path only the crawler's own Allow reopens (an
\verb{Allow: /company} under \verb{Disallow: /}) is left to
\code{allow_reopens_wildcard_disallow}. A group
of only Allows warns too. An explicit grant (\verb{Allow: /}, or an empty
\verb{Disallow:}, with no other Disallow) lifts every \code{*} Disallow but is almost
always deliberate, so it is reported as info. The crawlers that ignore
\verb{User-agent: *} whether or not a group names them get no warning, and
their info row says the \code{*} rules never applied to them.

\code{allow_reopens_wildcard_disallow} reports a \code{*} Disallow when a path is
disallowed by it under the \code{*} groups, allowed under the crawler's merged
groups, and disallowed by those groups' Disallows alone, so an Allow of
the crawler's reopens it. The candidate paths are the witness paths of
the \code{*} Disallow, those of the crawler's Allows, and the \code{*} Disallow read
under the literal prefix of each Allow (\verb{/creativegroup/_print.html} for
\verb{Allow: /creativegroup/} and \verb{Disallow: /*_print.html}). Google's matcher
decides every read, so a reported path is exact; an overlap no candidate
shows is skipped. A \code{*} Disallow the crawler loses outright is
\code{wildcard_disallows_not_inherited}'s, not this code's. When every path is
reopened by an every-path Allow (\verb{Disallow: /} with \verb{Allow: /}), the group
grants everything on purpose, so the row is info.

The \code{ai_training_} codes follow
draft-kelley-aipref-training-preference-signal-00 (dated 2026-10-30), an
Internet-Draft: a \verb{User-agent: __usage_ai-training} group states an
AI-training preference, a disallowed path one against training on it and
an allowed path one in favor. The draft matches the token exactly, apart
from ASCII case, and a \verb{User-agent: *} group never applies to it, so the
token is never reported as \code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited}, nor is a value
Google's matcher reads as the token (\verb{__usage_ai-training/1.0}). A near
miss is a value whose leading run of letters, \verb{_} and \code{-}, with the \verb{_}
and \code{-} removed, is \code{usageaitraining} in any case. The Google and Yandex
parsers join User-agent lines that no Allow or Disallow line separates
into one group; the Bing parser joins them only across blank and comment
lines and ends the group at any other directive
(\code{design/engine-disagreements.md} classes 1 and 1b). So a training
opt-out after a \code{*} group that holds no rule can become a Disallow for
every crawler, and \code{ai_training_token_stacked} says so, naming which
parsers join the lines. Another policy token or crawler a directive line
separates from the token is reported too. Blank and comment lines
separate nothing, and the draft lets a group mix tokens, so consecutive
or comment-headed User-agent lines that name crawlers stay silent. A
line after a token group's last rule may head the next group, so
\code{ai_training_group_extra_line} leaves it out unless the group is the
file's last. A key such as \code{DisallowAITraining} is
\code{accepted_directive_prefix}. A body cut at \code{max_bytes} is checked, and
the info then says the lines past the cut were not read. On a document
holding a NUL byte they follow Google's reading, as the other group
checks do.
Which crawler operators read the token, and from when, is recorded with
dates in \code{design/engine-profiles.md} section 7a.

For \code{user_agent_joined_across_directive} and
\code{user_agent_joined_across_blank}, Google's parser starts a new group only
after an Allow or Disallow line, so every User-agent line before a group's
first rule joins that group, and its rules reach every agent named there.
Consecutive User-agent lines are the deliberate way to share rules and stay
silent. A break between two User-agent lines of one group is reported on
the later line: a warning when a directive (\code{Sitemap}, \code{Crawl-delay},
\code{Host}, \code{Clean-param}, \code{Content-Signal} or any unknown field) lies between,
since Bing ends the group at the directive (\code{design/engine-disagreements.md}
class 1); an info when only blank or comment lines do, which Google and Bing
join and Yandex's tester splits; it names only the agents since the last
directive, since Bing ended the group of any agent before it. Yandex is
named only for a blank, comment or \code{Crawl-delay} line, the lines its
tester was seen to end a group at; its reading of a Sitemap or unknown
field is untested. A policy-token line is
left to \code{ai_training_token_stacked} and is never named as an earlier agent;
a line naming no crawler, a line whose only earlier agents share its name
and a group with no rule or only empty ones draw nothing, and the info is
left out when a directive follows later in the run. For agents not meant
to get the rules, the message suggests an empty \verb{Disallow:} ending their
group; moving a Sitemap out of the run is offered only to agents meant to
share them, since it makes the User-agent lines adjacent. Each message
names at most five earlier agents and three directive lines, and counts
the rest. On a document
holding a NUL byte the check follows Google's reading, as the other group
checks do.

For \code{user_agent_repeated_group} and \code{empty_disallow_with_full_block}, every
engine merges the groups that name one crawler (\code{design/engine-profiles.md}
section 5e): a repeat is reported once per later group with a rule and
crawler, and a line a \verb{user_agent_joined_*}, \code{user_agent_cut} or
\code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited} report already owns, or a policy-token
line, draws nothing but still counts for later groups. The warning fires
within one group too, where the engines split the same way. An every-path
Allow beside \verb{Disallow: /} is no split (every engine allows) and draws
neither.

For \code{disallow_tied_by_allow}, an Allow and a Disallow with the same
pattern match the same paths with the same priority, and Allow wins the
tie (RFC 9309 section 2.2.2), so the Disallow never applies; with
\verb{Allow: /}, a \verb{Disallow: /} blocks nothing. The comparison runs on the
Google parser's canonical form of each value (\verb{\%aa} equals \verb{\%AA}, and a
raw non-ASCII byte equals its \verb{\%XX} escape), with no \code{*} or \code{$} stripping.
Groups that name the same crawler are combined, a \verb{User-agent: *} Allow
never cancels a named crawler's Disallow, and a Disallow is reported only
when every crawler its group names has the identical Allow. Bing and
Yandex also let Allow win an identical-pattern tie. The check also runs
on a body cut at \code{max_bytes}: lines past the cut cannot revive the
Disallow.

For \code{duplicate_rule}, two rules are the same when type and canonical
value are byte-identical, as for \code{disallow_tied_by_allow}, so \verb{/x} and
\verb{/x*} are not duplicates. Groups that name the same crawler are combined,
\code{*} and named crawlers are kept apart, and a rule is reported only when
every crawler its group names already has it from an earlier line. Empty
values and patterns that never match are skipped, and a body cut at
\code{max_bytes} is checked too. A group's only Allow or Disallow line is not
reported when a User-agent line follows it: that line keeps the next
User-agent line from joining its group. The canonical value has its
escapes in upper case, so a line that differs only by a lower-case escape
from a line in the same group that writes the escape in upper case is the
same rule. It is reported as \code{rule_lowercase_escape_twin} instead, and it
neither gets a \code{duplicate_rule} row nor counts as the earlier rule of
one. In other groups, such a line keeps \code{rule_lowercase_escape} and may
get \code{duplicate_rule}.

On a document holding a NUL byte, \code{wildcard_rules_not_inherited},
\code{disallow_tied_by_allow}, \code{duplicate_rule} and the AI-training codes
follow Google's reading: the parser and Google's matcher end a line at its
first NUL, while the Bing and Yandex matchers read the NUL as an ordinary
byte, so each such message says it is Google's reading, unless every NUL
sits in a comment. \code{user_agent_cut} runs too; its message already names
Google's matcher.

The validator adds three info codes for text that may have
been damaged on its way into the file. They are advisories, not validity
findings: they change no status, no parse and no match, and each message
says the character may be meant. Some damage is already visible without
them: mixed UTF-8 and Latin-1 bytes are usually malformed UTF-8, which
\code{invalid_utf8} reports. Text that is valid UTF-8 can neither prove nor
disprove corruption, so the check reads only a closed set of indicators
in the raw value of an active Allow or Disallow line, as the parser reads
it (cut at the first \verb{#}, at a NUL and at the line buffer):
\code{rule_replacement_character} for U+FFFD; \code{rule_invisible_character} for
U+00AD, U+200B to U+200F, U+202A to U+202E, U+2060 to U+2064, U+2066 to
U+2069, U+FEFF and the C1 controls U+0080 to U+009F; and
\code{rule_possible_mojibake} for two or more sequences of a Latin-1
character U+00C2 to U+00F4 followed by the U+0080 to U+00BF characters
that, read back as bytes, make one well-formed UTF-8 character (U+00C3
U+00A9 for an e-acute). The mojibake check is a heuristic and can flag
intended text: \code{2} U+00D7 U+00BD (a multiplication sign and a vulgar
half) twice in one value, or capitals such as U+00C9 each followed by a
no-break space U+00A0, read the same way. A single such sequence is not
reported as mojibake; one whose second character is U+0080 to U+009F is
still a C1 control and gets \code{rule_invisible_character}. No other
non-ASCII character is matched, so most paths with accented letters, a
path in Japanese, or a Latin-1 string converted to UTF-8 on input stay
silent, as do a typed escape such as \verb{\%E2\%80\%8B}, a comment, a
User-agent or Sitemap value, and a rule value that is not valid UTF-8
(bytes after the cut, in a comment or past a NUL, do not count; a
character the line buffer splits is dropped). Each message quotes code
points as \code{U+XXXX} with byte offsets counted from 1 at the value's first
byte after the blanks that follow the colon, listing at most ten, and a
line gets at most one row per code. A U+200B or U+FEFF that leads the
value is left to \code{rule_missing_leading_slash}, which names it. A document
gets at most 100 such rows; the last one counts the rest. Only the
Latin-1 mojibake pattern is read: a Windows-1252 decoding, NFC or NFD
normalization and the intended original text are out of scope, and
nothing is repaired.
}

\examples{
report <- robots_validate_text(
  "user-agent: *\ndisallow: /private\n"
)
report$documents
report$diagnostics

malformed <- robots_validate_text(charToRaw(
  "disallow: /before-agent\nunknown-field: value"
))
malformed$diagnostics

}
